06639b8d-d03d-4abc-a97d-560c88e8de31	[Context: cbc > esr | cleveland]\nYour provider will time how long it takes your red blood cells to settle to the bottom of the test tube.\n\nIf it happens faster than usual, you might have an inflammatory condition.\n\nYou don’t need to do anything special to get ready for a sed rate test. You should be able to eat, drink fluids and take all of your usual medications and supplements.\n\nA provider or phlebotomist will wipe an antiseptic on your arm and insert a needle into your vein. They may ask you to make a fist to help blood flow to that arm.\n\nYou might feel a sharp pinch when your provider inserts the needle. Tell them if you’re nervous about needles or blood tests. They’ll help you relax and stay calm.\n\nThe sed rate test is extremely safe and doesn’t have any risk of complications. It doesn’t cause side effects.\n\nYour arm might hurt for a few hours after your test. Your provider will put a bandage on the spot where they inserted the needle. It’s normal to have minor bleeding or bruising after a blood draw.\n\nAn ESR test shows how quickly your red blood cells sink to the bottom of your blood sample. Most providers use the Westergren method. They’ll put a sample of your blood into a special vial called a Westergren tube. Then, they’ll see how long it takes your red blood cells to sink to the bottom.\n\nYour provider will measure the rate of red blood cell sedimentation as a speed — millimeters per hour (mm/hr). It’s like a tiny version of your car’s speedometer measuring how fast you’re driving in miles or kilometers per hour.\n\nA normal range for ESR depends on your age and sex:\n\nAge and sex\nNormal ESR\n\nMales younger than 50\nLess than 15 millimeters per hour (<15 mm/hr)\n\nMales older than 50\n<20mm/hr\n\nFemales younger than 50\n<20mm/hr\n\nFemales older than 50\n<30mm/hr\n\nNewborn babies\n0 to 2 mm/hr\n\nChildren who haven’t hit puberty yet\n3 to 13 mm/hr\n\nAge and sex\n\nMales younger than 50\n\nNormal ESR\n\nLess than 15 millimeters per hour (<15 mm/hr)\n\nMales older than 50\n\nNormal ESR\n\n<20mm/hr\n\nFemales younger than 50\n\nNormal ESR\n\n<20mm/hr\n\nFemales older than 50	{"url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diagnostics/17747-sed-rate-erythrocyte-sedimentation-rate-or-esr-test", "title": "ESR (Erythrocyte Sedimentation Rate)", "topic": "esr", "source": "Cleveland Clinic", "category": "cbc", "file_path": "cbc/esr/clean/cleveland/v1.md", "chunk_index": 1, "total_chunks": 4, "source_org_code": "cleveland", "parent_document_id": "f4be5342-702d-463c-bb9e-1ce2cf8e2c1e"}	[0.030546742,-0.013357533,-0.005523092,-0.065490454,-0.007613728,0.0067782514,0.015890166,0.0294834,0.0061201337,0.017750427,-0.0032289922,-0.019749478,-0.028360859,0.009653713,0.103452034,0.0068083415,0.00886377,-0.013864847,0.0037472479,-0.019441107,0.0058126245,-0.0015368359,-0.00046728973,-0.0026272426,0.0028010798,-0.004893446,-0.0033062433,-0.033105586,0.05572607,0.013358054,-0.0064258073,-0.02046517,0.03010735,0.025253516,0.0065758824,0.022560488,0.0011299758,-0.021972511,0.0026876342,0.026032392,0.0023293355,0.0005231886,-0.0064433906,-0.016126024,0.028489057,-0.0077785226,0.0048448793,-0.0014201246,0.024925897,0.016203739,0.010307088,-0.00022532513,-0.00021135662,-0.19377178,0.011183835,0.012998516,0.040203165,-0.016792031,-0.013900451,0.0031821595,-0.016361043,0.0071864664,-0.016094895,-0.0031427487,-0.010019462,-0.014477166,0.009835267,-0.00024120121,-0.009224829,0.00090984534,0.005986184,0.015245627,-0.0037155936,-0.019267239,0.0009021322,-0.021541292,0.0034031763,0.017255407,0.002093648,-0.00264166,-0.022417601,0.006423831,-0.010008714,-0.008413447,-0.015238773,0.010902861,-0.022795951,0.031830657,0.0036952407,-0.0078014107,-0.014977845,0.02074878,0.032395113,-0.01929627,-0.00074330595,0.0220393,0.0015778955,-0.00014532884,-0.0030720863,-0.021053849,-0.037134986,-0.00047671166,-0.011126969,-0.029557943,-0.0013013012,-0.015692377,0.012700641,0.015698135,0.010948156,-0.007941719