552a3a32-d752-4386-a048-ccfc5e0428ec	[Context: medications > insulin_therapy | mayo]\nAdults should try to get 7 to 9 hours of sleep every night. Let your healthcare professional know if you often have trouble sleeping. Finding and treating the cause can help improve sleep. But if you don't have sleep apnea or restless legs syndrome, follow these simple tips for getting more restful sleep.\n\nStick to a sleep schedule. Go to bed and wake up at the same time each day. Try to keep the same schedule on weeknights and on weekends.\nMake where you sleep restful. Keep the sleeping space cool, quiet and dark. Do something that relaxes you in the hour before bedtime. That might include taking a warm bath or doing relaxation exercises. Turn off screens such as your TV, phone and computer screen.\nWatch what you eat and drink. Don't go to bed hungry or too full. Try not to have large meals close to bedtime. And don't use nicotine, caffeine and alcohol close to bedtime.\nTake shorter naps. If napping during the day helps you, keep naps to 30 minutes or less, and take them earlier in the day. You may sleep better at night.\n\n8. Ease stress\n\nLong-term stress may play a part in high blood pressure. But more research is needed to tell if lowering stress can help bring down blood pressure.\n\nThe American Heart Association suggests meditation and deep breathing exercises to help lower stress.\n\nConnecting with friends and family also may help lower stress. Think about joining a support group. You may find that talking about your health concerns with others in similar situations can help.\n\n9. Track your blood pressure at home and get regular checkups\n\nCheck your blood pressure at home to help make sure that your lifestyle changes are working.\n\nYou can buy a home blood pressure monitor without a prescription at many stores. Talk with a healthcare professional about checking your blood pressure at home before you start. Learn how to check your blood pressure. And track your blood pressure so you can see the pattern over time.\n\nRegular visits with a healthcare professional are an important part of blood pressure management. Ask your healthcare professional how often you need checkups.\n\n10. Manage your cholesterol and blood sugar\n\nHigh blood sugar and high levels of "bad" non-HDL cholesterol do not affect blood pressure directly. But they do raise the risk of heart disease. Non-HDL cholesterol is your total cholesterol minus your "good" HDL cholesterol.\n\nSome of the same healthy habits that help lower blood pressure also help manage cholesterol and blood sugar.	{"url": "https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/diabetes/in-depth/insulin-and-diabetes/art-20046974", "title": "Insulin Therapy", "topic": "insulin_therapy", "source": "Mayo Clinic", "category": "medications", "file_path": "medications/insulin_therapy/clean/mayo/v1.md", "chunk_index": 2, "total_chunks": 4, "source_org_code": "mayo", "parent_document_id": "55834105-eb75-4696-a2df-420eed0fed11"}	[0.012161243,0.009971707,-0.01919932,-0.06166445,0.017732352,0.016614325,0.027814638,0.051582128,0.012969332,0.0020601724,0.003900763,-0.006113182,0.00729082,-0.0066514276,0.11020096,0.040720716,-0.013025014,-0.009328564,-0.0052861106,-0.021765836,0.0264251,-0.027402699,-0.004645708,-0.017520195,0.015705792,-0.0024115753,0.011571742,0.0157766,0.007585707,0.016321419,0.013058026,0.02301163,0.017439084,0.037412953,0.0133452695,0.0106730405,-0.014105053,-0.023068488,-0.00080613344,0.010229327,0.02897679,0.011910283,6.468218e-05,0.007368621,0.012911539,-0.0019527249,0.020658532,0.011668445,0.017318923,0.018841613,0.009115606,-0.011329626,-0.048429497,-0.19247456,0.0064191553,-0.02671417,0.0030108178,-0.0103543885,0.01074131,-0.014164571,-0.014874432,0.01997315,0.006151161,-0.00488944,-0.00077531603,-0.022542814,0.028564041,-0.0016544366,0.012606647,-0.047122736,0.009062648,0.007531449,-0.009822633,0.010992762,-0.014440814,-0.023790095,-0.013808589,0.004661379,0.0072002453,-0.0140