8bfee444-5bd3-43ea-b17e-bc0954a6af74	[Context: diabetes > insulin | mayo]\nTalk with a healthcare professional about setting up an exercise program for you.\n\n4. Lower sodium in your diet\n\nLess sodium in the diet can improve heart health and blood pressure in most people. Sodium's effect on blood pressure varies among groups of people. Limit sodium to 2,300 milligrams (mg) a day or less. But for most adults, limiting sodium to 1,500 mg a day or less is better. Doing that may lower high blood pressure by about 5 to 6 mm Hg.\n\nTo lower sodium in the diet:\n\nRead food labels. Look for low-sodium or no-sodium foods and drinks.\nEat fewer processed foods. Only a small amount of sodium is natural in foods. Most sodium is added during processing.\nDon't add table salt. Use herbs or spices to add flavor to food.\nCook your own food. Cooking your food lets you manage how much sodium you put in it.\nUse a potassium-based salt substitute. This not only helps lower sodium but adds potassium to your diet.\n\n5. Limit alcohol\n\nDrinking too much alcohol can make your blood pressure go up. And alcohol can keep blood pressure medicines from working well.\n\nIf you choose to drink alcohol, limit how often you drink and keep the amount small. For overall health, drink one or fewer drinks a day. Do not drink every day. The less you drink, the better. One drink equals 12 fluid ounces of beer, 5 ounces of wine or 1.5 ounces of 80-proof liquor.\n\n6. Don't smoke or vape\n\nOne of the best things you can do for your heart health is to stay away from tobacco or nicotine products. This includes electronic cigarettes and vaping devices. Tobacco and nicotine raise blood pressure. Not using these products may lower your risk of heart disease and improve overall health.\n\nAlso stay away from the smoke from a burning cigarette or pipe and the smoke a tobacco user breathes out. Such smoke is called secondhand smoke.\n\nIf you need help quitting, talk with your healthcare team about programs or treatments that can help.\n\n7. Get a good night's sleep\n\nGetting fewer than seven hours of sleep every night for weeks can affect blood pressure. Conditions that can disturb sleep include sleep apnea, restless legs syndrome and sleeplessness, also called insomnia. Being overweight also can cause pauses in breathing while you sleep, a condition called sleep apnea. Sleep apnea raises blood pressure.	{"url": "https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/diabetes/in-depth/insulin-and-diabetes/art-20046974", "title": "Insulin", "topic": "insulin", "source": "Mayo Clinic", "category": "diabetes", "file_path": "diabetes/insulin/clean/mayo/v1.md", "chunk_index": 1, "total_chunks": 4, "source_org_code": "mayo", "parent_document_id": "1690867f-3fb4-4f5d-bbc2-43ae684875b5"}	[0.024929382,0.0004613524,-0.01225804,-0.060954247,0.016824497,-0.007333372,0.03534171,0.028737409,0.027852394,0.004190905,-0.005252482,-0.00051840564,0.0036087842,0.0091876015,0.11718153,0.033018116,0.0011227172,-0.024893237,-0.00032189986,-0.018276397,0.0062422296,-0.012076365,0.013717644,-0.019023495,0.017871642,-0.01418175,0.013045076,0.009261756,0.013065667,0.029457489,0.03174574,0.010464807,0.023873284,0.019190742,0.03536289,0.03459231,-0.0009065696,-0.030161748,0.0043955534,0.011050427,0.01653755,0.0047359834,0.010730085,-0.0052454523,0.00926639,-0.0030378841,0.022747573,0.013235303,0.014607425,0.013621375,0.016447129,-0.01948122,-0.059126742,-0.18213566,-0.003170619,-0.007707452,-0.0100953095,0.0065909643,0.009169427,-0.026145374,-0.013142552,0.02157066,0.012878135,0.004108817,-0.009385538,-0.020112483,0.018346015,-0.0017070972,0.015105124,-0.03639337,0.002853228,0.0073367557,-0.009686013,0.014813808,-0.023127535,-0.0064514983,-0.006004599,0.010738314,-0.002529555,-0.016060304,-0.03229697,-0.03618498,-0.00081846275,-0.017914483,-0.021745155,-0.010280397,0.016502097,0.017323794,0.0010899256,0.02053082,0.016887877,0.018785648,0.017356802,0.012040035,-0.006248612,-0.009473248,0.001167085,-0.0