6d26be88-b40c-4056-a2a6-7d7f5345fa42	[Context: diagnosis > average_blood_glucose | ada]\nYou may have it more often if your treatment plan has changed or isn’t working as expected.\n\nA1C and Diabetes Diagnosis\n\nThe A1C test is often used to diagnose prediabetes or diabetes. If you’re tested this way, you don't have to fast or drink anything before being tested.\n\nUnderstanding Why A1C Is Important in Diabetes Management\n\nMonitoring your blood glucose at home and your A1C results are two key parts of managing diabetes. Both give you information about your blood glucose levels and if your treatment plan is working.\n\nWhen you use a blood glucose meter (BGM) or a continuous glucose monitor (CGM), you’re getting information about your blood glucose level at that moment (CGMs give you more information along with it). This allows you to make choices to manage it based on your treatment plan. It also can highlight if there’s an ongoing issue at a specific time during your day or week.\n\nYour A1C offers a big picture of how your blood glucose has been over time. This allows you and your healthcare team to talk about if changes to your treatment plan are needed. These could be changes to your eating plan, physical activity, emotional support, or the medications you take. Reaching your A1C targets is one way to lower your risk of developing diabetes complications.\n\nWhat should my A1C target be?\n\nFor most people living with diabetes, an A1C of 7% or less is the recommended target. Your A1C target will be based on your needs and goals.\n\nUnderstanding A1C Results\n\nA1C test results are reported as a percentage. The higher the percentage, the higher your blood glucose levels have been over the past 2 to 3 months. Your A1C result can be shown as estimated average glucose (eAG) using mg/dL, the same way you see the result of your blood glucose checks.\n\nChanging Your A1C to eAG\n\nIf your lab test results or your healthcare team haven’t given you your eAG, you can use the following calculator or chart to get your eAG based on your A1C results. You can also see which A1C would give you a different eAG.\n\nTechnology has become an essential part of diabetes management. Compare and learn about continuous glucose monitors (CGMs), insulin pumps, blood glucose meters, and more in the new Consumer Guide!	{"url": "https://diabetes.org/about-diabetes/a1c", "title": "Average Blood Glucose", "topic": "average_blood_glucose", "source": "American Diabetes Association", "category": "diagnosis", "file_path": "diagnosis/average_blood_glucose/clean/ada/v1.md", "chunk_index": 1, "total_chunks": 3, "source_org_code": "ada", "parent_document_id": "3ce46173-a24b-4869-ba81-42f98a7720a1"}	[0.025048465,-0.0037625234,-0.023604892,-0.059052512,-0.0011236466,-0.023291728,0.025650287,0.018163212,0.007096467,-0.0061177253,0.012372763,-0.01147694,-0.009317126,0.0036889266,0.113319784,0.021241935,0.009465817,0.012604576,0.006888762,-0.007004388,-0.006890515,0.023309173,0.006512419,-0.010059763,0.00032148784,0.002355211,-0.008206497,0.0010130673,0.037004672,0.018037716,-0.0074592596,-0.011188501,0.033720564,0.005716262,0.004186908,0.012749555,-0.0032485025,-0.0026377772,0.016780315,-0.016974857,-0.017364684,0.009645624,0.0032734284,-0.0013115964,0.0076614376,-0.007701949,-0.018140674,0.007596823,0.01870447,0.019214224,-0.0057037077,0.0030987328,0.0016752158,-0.17597787,0.012355272,-0.016439175,-0.016810048,0.015791286,-0.007846291,0.00525803,-0.04035179,-7.4383046e-05,-0.006667636,-0.015790433,-0.013415464,-0.040284015,0.006603839,-0.012168623,-0.031034347,-0.03961992,0.015169709,-0.01876584,0.006390402,-0.025051283,-0.004658082,0.005246951,0.007197529,-0.012183364,-0.0011811908,-0.010496911,-0.02647476,-0.01707113,-0.008826004,0.0062318104,0.005332964,-0.015480102,-0.025952853,-0.017021524,-0.0038032664,-0.0032732321,-0.026660262,0.03026941,0.001368678,-0.0077030575,-0.015547763,-0.03403289,0.0020612455,-0.03021789,0.0042061997,-0.0018666273,-0.007044519,-0