ce551c7c-4a96-4220-80b9-f37fd693f2a8	[Context: lifestyle > stress_management | mayo]\nBut when stressors are always present and you always feel stressed, that fight-or-flight reaction stays turned on. This may happen when you are dealing with chronic stress or worrying about the future.\n\nWhen the stress response system is active for too long, it can give you too much exposure to cortisol and other stress hormones. That can disrupt almost all the body's processes. This puts you at higher risk of many health conditions.\n\nMental health effects include:\n\nAnxiety. Frequent worry can cause many of the same health issues as stress.\nDepression. Long-term stress can lead to feelings of hopelessness and helplessness.\nIssues with memory and focus. High levels of the stress hormone cortisol can make it hard to focus and remember things. This can make it harder to think.\n\nShort-term physical health effects include:\n\nDigestive problems. Stomach pain and bloating are two ways stress may affect your gut. You also may get heartburn or acid reflux.\nMuscle tension and pain. Stress causes muscles to get tense. When stress is long-term, your muscles tend to always be tight. Tight muscles can cause other issues, including neck and back pain.\nHeadaches. Muscle tightness can lead to headaches. A headache may be a physical sign telling you that you're stressed.\nSleep difficulties. Racing thoughts and anxiety can affect how much and how well you sleep.\n\nLong-term physical health effects include:\n\nShortness of breath and rapid breathing. This can worsen conditions such as asthma and chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD).\nWeight gain. When you're stressed, you may not eat as well or get enough regular physical activity. Stress also can change the way your body functions in ways that can lead to weight gain.\nSexual issues. You may have a lower sexual interest. Stress also can affect the ability to conceive. Stress also can worsen symptoms of menstruation and menopause.\nHeart disease, heart attack, high blood pressure and stroke. Over time, chronic stress can raise the heart rate and lead to issues with the blood vessels. These issues can lead to conditions such as heart disease and stroke.\n\nBecause stress can affect your health in so many ways, it's important to learn healthy ways to cope with things that bring you stress.\n\nWhy you react to stress the way you do\n\nYour reaction to a potentially stressful event is different from everyone else's. How you react to your life stressors is affected by your genetics and your life experiences.	{"url": "https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/diabetes/in-depth/stress-and-diabetes/art-20046037", "title": "Stress Management", "topic": "stress_management", "source": "Mayo Clinic", "category": "lifestyle", "file_path": "lifestyle/stress_management/clean/mayo/v1.md", "chunk_index": 1, "total_chunks": 5, "source_org_code": "mayo", "parent_document_id": "9a0d8028-433c-4abb-8935-2101c6f342b5"}	[0.006277995,0.0022448914,-0.0005751683,-0.020375127,0.018591484,0.013306066,0.024363529,0.019417971,0.015531391,0.03894506,-0.017283969,0.0026018105,-0.0039443984,0.016977739,0.12678544,0.0026525825,0.0011815674,0.013939244,0.015047541,0.001231775,0.02290141,-0.01019024,-0.003470052,-0.0075877253,0.004214083,-0.0059981383,0.018265508,0.00017901327,0.023160756,-0.0015212097,0.028045705,-0.0011462758,0.0014925014,-0.0052570105,0.00270831,0.020955794,0.015516027,-0.033241007,-0.011495561,0.02137504,0.026138017,-0.0025591853,0.016422031,0.0011344553,0.023890862,-0.0075693727,0.012222415,0.021008113,0.018135684,0.015062729,-0.015086159,0.003713762,-0.023463376,-0.18687335,0.013748435,-0.027430156,-0.0088416245,-0.011600314,0.01765077,-0.011662563,0.0059230905,0.04707689,-0.008879285,-0.0042720665,0.0072816447,-0.008351796,0.046595626,0.008835353,-0.022357823,-0.01126278,0.006250243,0.007940683,-0.008048789,-0.0023668327,-0.008511657,0.008659701,0.018232873,0.0013539548,-0.011116613,-0.013445134,-