442bc6fb-7302-4151-a8c9-91006658922b	[Context: lifestyle > stress_management | mayo]\nThe demands of life also play a role.\n\nGenes. Your genes help shape how you react to stress. This is why some people are naturally laid-back, while others tend to respond quickly to even the slightest stressor.\nLife experiences. Your life experiences also shape how you respond to stressors. For example, people who have experienced trauma may react more strongly to stress than those who have not. Examples of trauma include experiencing abuse or neglect as a child or being in a car accident. People in the military, police officers, firefighters, and people who have lived through violent crime all may react more strongly to stress based on their experiences.\nLife's demands. Day-to-day pressures also can put you in a constant state of alert. Thinking about what happened during the day or worrying about what might happen tomorrow can make your brain think it needs to be on guard all the time. This can lead to changes in your brain that keep you constantly focused on the things that are causing you stress.\n\nLearning to react to stress in a healthy way\n\nStress is not just about what is happening to you. It also depends on how you react to stressors. If you don't make changes, long-lasting stress, called chronic stress, can build up. That can lead to problems such as missing work, avoiding people or feeling upset easily. A healthy way to deal with stress is to focus on what you can control. For example, you might ask for help, do fewer tasks or let go of trying to be perfect when you can. Taking small steps such as these can help you feel less overwhelmed.\n\nYour thoughts also affect how you feel and act. If you always think the worst is going to happen, your stress grows. Instead, try to think in a more balanced way. Remind yourself of what is realistic. Accept what you can't change. And if your mind starts to spiral, shift your focus to something else. Over time, thinking this way and making steady choices can help lower your stress and improve how you handle it.\n\nStressful events are facts of life. And you may not be able to change your current situation. But these steps can help you manage the impact that stressful events have on you.\n\nIn addition to approaching stress in a healthy way, there are also plenty of simple, everyday ways you can cope with stress.\n\nTry these stress management tips:\n\nStart with the basics. Eat a healthy diet and get regular exercise. Get plenty of sleep too.	{"url": "https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/diabetes/in-depth/stress-and-diabetes/art-20046037", "title": "Stress Management", "topic": "stress_management", "source": "Mayo Clinic", "category": "lifestyle", "file_path": "lifestyle/stress_management/clean/mayo/v1.md", "chunk_index": 2, "total_chunks": 5, "source_org_code": "mayo", "parent_document_id": "9a0d8028-433c-4abb-8935-2101c6f342b5"}	[0.00044769802,0.0031562513,0.011261722,-0.0033329306,0.02373137,0.015691487,0.028762888,0.03664464,0.01784696,0.027734054,-0.013953301,-0.0043646446,9.416099e-05,0.016488181,0.13153543,0.020340612,-0.00615491,-0.0035497488,0.028399415,0.012096938,0.00035296008,-0.00980395,-0.0011200014,-0.011594043,-0.0027882133,-0.015288895,0.012111199,0.0001846771,0.026360812,0.0105718225,0.024742661,0.016722828,0.004586504,0.007933405,-0.004714342,0.011590994,0.00687482,-0.02450779,-0.014168758,0.010245591,0.01369489,0.005532269,0.008349498,-0.01445224,0.0078291,-0.028492488,0.016042084,-0.006822312,0.0044040247,-0.006575876,-0.007936813,0.002060318,-0.013472897,-0.19030488,-0.012666255,-0.034059677,-0.011653137,-0.023655314,0.01074525,-0.0036787372,0.001513071,0.02232186,0.025083251,-0.010798248,0.043992426,-0.013151895,0.030055398,0.014641802,-0.03069168,-0.006559215,-0.004744355,0.019404873,-0.018219642,-0.008359002,-0.0037792618,0.006771987,0.0013355482,0.0075510074,-0.028766276,-0.030782683,0.0071725566,-0.031536683,-0.007295521,-0.018014895,0.0032254234,-0.0