88b23c90-24d5-4693-936a-ab07f7791205	[Context: lifestyle > stress_management | mayo]\nDo relaxation exercises such as yoga, deep breathing, massage or meditation.\nKeep a journal. Write about your thoughts or what you're grateful for in your life. Your journal also may be a place where you write down what's causing your stress and ways you might manage it.\nTurn off screens before sleep. Give your eyes and mind a break from computers and other devices for at least an hour before you go to bed. A break from social media is especially important.\nTake time for hobbies, such as reading or listening to music. Or watch your favorite show or movie.\nFoster healthy friendships, and talk with friends and family who care about and support you.\nHave a sense of humor. Find ways to include humor and laughter in your life, such as watching funny movies or looking at joke websites.\nVolunteer in your community.\nOrganize and focus on what you need to get done at home and work and remove tasks that aren't needed.\nFind new ways to look at challenges. You can't always get rid of stress, but by thinking about a stressful situation differently, you can change how it affects you.\nSeek professional counseling. A counselor can help you learn specific coping skills to manage stress.\n\nTry not to rely on ways of coping that can harm your health, such as using alcohol, tobacco or drugs, or overeating to cope. If you're worried that your use of these products has gone up or changed due to stress, talk to your healthcare professional.\n\nWhen to ask for help\n\nIf you’ve taken steps to manage your stress but you keep having symptoms, see your healthcare professional. Your healthcare professional may want to check for other potential causes. You also could see a counselor or therapist. This person can help you find sources of stress and learn new coping skills. And if you are concerned about harming yourself, call 911 or a suicide hotline. In the U.S., call or text 988 to reach the 988 Suicide & Crisis Lifeline. Or use the Lifeline Chat.\n\nAlso, get emergency help right away if you have chest pain, especially if you also have shortness of breath, sweating, dizziness, nausea, or jaw, back, shoulder or arm pain. These may be warning signs of a heart attack and not simply stress symptoms.\n\nLiving well with stress\n\nTaking time to lower your stress can help you feel better today and stay healthier over time. Research shows that feeling happier and more relaxed can benefit your health in many ways.	{"url": "https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/diabetes/in-depth/stress-and-diabetes/art-20046037", "title": "Stress Management", "topic": "stress_management", "source": "Mayo Clinic", "category": "lifestyle", "file_path": "lifestyle/stress_management/clean/mayo/v1.md", "chunk_index": 3, "total_chunks": 5, "source_org_code": "mayo", "parent_document_id": "9a0d8028-433c-4abb-8935-2101c6f342b5"}	[0.0044531818,-0.0028654868,-0.019836714,-0.020063376,0.028058337,0.010708535,0.03758259,0.01596354,0.0060869325,0.023359895,-0.014415311,-0.0056304373,0.018111782,0.010922234,0.12541953,0.0024401115,-0.00428094,0.0040726396,0.028979564,0.0030801718,0.0038936648,-0.015976885,-0.018636266,-0.01156909,0.010943127,-0.009361799,0.03030618,-0.0074603995,0.026731623,0.022436948,0.03760098,0.018840676,0.010326299,0.022932982,0.029576544,0.014299949,0.0060591237,-0.032084975,-0.0046259253,0.02135583,0.017780598,0.011534679,0.02377399,-0.013926598,0.030412836,-0.00047760506,0.023633705,-0.0011828947,0.0060629705,0.0089490395,0.0062816036,0.014666111,-0.038915165,-0.1914308,0.0061522024,-0.010253338,-0.013362085,-0.014591765,0.01711155,0.0012480645,-0.010307066,0.023595527,0.0072368323,-0.007394961,0.021163646,0.008882238,0.024870275,-0.0014547767,-0.0066862986,-0.023045547,-0.008553926,0.022934455,-0.0122825885,0.005488965,-0.000667647,-0.00666738,-0.011914889,0.022322297,0.0031825344,-0.008754588,-0.0018694124,-0.008877561,-0.014746875,0.0071312124,0.0033715898,-0.00