9dd97498-4b49-48e6-9963-b95c8ad9503c	[Context: lifestyle > stress_management | cdc]\nWhat to know\n\nHaving diabetes can be overwhelming at times. The good news is that there are things you can do to cope with diabetes and manage stress.\n\nManaging diabetes can be hard\n\nSometimes you may feel overwhelmed. You need to check your blood sugar levels often, make healthy food choices, and be physically active. You also need to remember to take your medicine and make other good decisions about your health throughout the day. You may also worry about having low or high blood sugar levels, the costs of your medicines, and developing diabetes-related complications.\n\nWhen all of this feels like too much to deal with, you may have something called diabetes distress. This is when all the worry, frustration, anger, and burnout makes it hard for you to keep up with the daily demands of diabetes.\n\nThe good news is that there are things you can do to cope with diabetes and manage stress. Here are 10 tips that can help.\n\n10 coping tips\n\nPay attention to your feelings. Almost everyone feels frustrated or stressed from time to time. Dealing with diabetes can add to these feelings and make you feel overwhelmed. Having these feelings for more than a week or two may signal that you need help so that you can feel better.\nTalk with your health care team about your feelings. Let your doctor, nurse, diabetes educator, psychologist, or social worker know how you've been feeling. They can help you problem-solve your concerns about diabetes. They may also suggest that you speak with other health care providers to get help.\nTalk to your health care team about negative reactions other people may have about your diabetes. They can help you manage feelings of being judged by others because you have diabetes. It's important not to feel that you have to hide your diabetes from other people.\nAsk if help is available for the costs of diabetes medicines and supplies. Talk with your pharmacist and other health care providers. They may know about programs that can help with costs. You can also check with community health centers. They may know about programs that help people get insulin, diabetes medicines, and supplies (test strips, syringes, etc.).\nTalk with your family and friends. Tell those closest to you how you feel about having diabetes. Be honest about the problems you're having in dealing with diabetes. Just telling others how you feel helps to relieve some of the stress. However, sometimes the people around you may add to your stress. Let them know how and when you need them to help you.	{"url": "https://www.cdc.gov/diabetes/articles/10-tips-coping-diabetes-distress.html", "title": "Stress Management", "topic": "stress_management", "source": "Centers for Disease Control and Prevention", "category": "lifestyle", "file_path": "lifestyle/stress_management/clean/cdc/v1.md", "chunk_index": 0, "total_chunks": 2, "source_org_code": "cdc", "parent_document_id": "6414a3f5-1696-4620-b8ef-26aec2a47cae"}	[0.0114058005,-0.01094554,-0.014848651,-0.0403723,-0.00087126263,0.0034353219,0.008385649,0.014053255,0.013119289,0.017434822,-0.019485695,-0.020341815,0.03570581,0.01846627,0.119317316,0.02252822,0.013339604,0.00635366,0.015525753,-0.0066072308,-0.017596934,-0.008552004,0.008769453,-0.033702422,0.026731307,-0.009139818,0.011989174,0.008647068,0.017809242,0.0227773,0.032578442,0.037658993,0.0013054068,-0.0010616601,0.01229325,-0.024441374,0.016082784,-0.012373945,0.0019769087,0.007124185,-0.003662077,0.027809238,0.016261749,-0.013268506,0.012376197,-0.015946645,0.004369615,-0.006185909,0.022305284,0.015543821,-0.02176065,0.001432683,-0.03012885,-0.18725647,-0.0155350175,-0.0070493035,-0.016853547,0.0055108177,-0.0013359683,-0.012637238,-0.0077239163,0.0179408,-0.0025083432,-0.0044323183,0.029778646,-0.019422246,0.019484162,-0.013999525,0.0014669666,-0.023602905,0.011611653,-0.008172755,-0.02032578,0.00798774,0.002715834,0.013340956,-0.019218497,-2.0812