f6fd38a5-09ae-4271-b2b8-7e27067057b8	[Context: lifestyle > stress_management | mayo]\nStress is part of life. Everyone has some amount of stress. Work demands, a family to care for and daily tasks are just a few sources of stress that may be part of your reality. This can lead to long-lasting stress, called chronic stress.\n\nEven if you feel like you handle stress well, your body takes note of all the stresses you face. Your body sees stress as a threat, even if you don't. That's because your brain is hard-wired to look for threats and protect you from them.\n\nThough your brain may think that you're under attack when you're faced with stress, you don't have to feel that way. You can learn to live well with stress.\n\nUnderstanding the natural stress response\n\nChronic stress can harm your body and mind by releasing stress hormones for too long. This can lead to issues such as headaches, poor sleep and heart disease. It also can weaken your immune system. And it can lead to changes in daily habits, such as getting less physical activity. This can make it harder for your body to stay healthy and recover from illness.\n\nWhen you face a perceived threat, a tiny area at the brain's base, called the hypothalamus, sets off an alarm system in the body. An example of a perceived threat is a large dog barking at you during your morning walk. Through nerve and hormonal signals, this alarm system prompts the adrenal glands, found atop the kidneys, to release a surge of hormones, such as adrenaline and cortisol.\n\nAdrenaline makes the heart beat faster, causes blood pressure to go up and gives you more energy. Cortisol, the main stress hormone, increases sugar, also called glucose, in the blood. It also helps the brain use glucose more effectively. It also increases the availability of substances in the body that repair tissues.\n\nCortisol also slows body functions that are not needed or harmful in a fight-or-flight situation. It changes immune system responses and makes it harder for the digestive system, the reproductive system and growth processes to work. This complex natural alarm system also communicates with the brain regions that control mood, motivation and fear.\n\nWhen the natural stress response becomes overactive\n\nThe body's stress response system usually helps you handle tough situations. It turns itself on and off as needed. Once a perceived threat has passed, hormones return to typical levels. As adrenaline and cortisol levels drop, your heart rate and blood pressure return to typical levels. Other systems go back to their regular activities.	{"url": "https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/diabetes/in-depth/stress-and-diabetes/art-20046037", "title": "Stress Management", "topic": "stress_management", "source": "Mayo Clinic", "category": "lifestyle", "file_path": "lifestyle/stress_management/clean/mayo/v1.md", "chunk_index": 0, "total_chunks": 5, "source_org_code": "mayo", "parent_document_id": "9a0d8028-433c-4abb-8935-2101c6f342b5"}	[0.0035390202,-0.0024002958,-0.008581602,-0.00762288,0.0021318344,0.007743748,0.021822209,0.029495694,0.02618974,0.028224584,-0.019414289,-0.013263558,-0.014258895,0.013676323,0.12379601,0.01238633,0.0076678414,0.010435898,0.010023695,-0.016656708,0.0031486424,-0.01960724,0.0003796575,-0.008649611,-0.013284768,-0.006190139,-0.0013998233,-0.0008688523,0.01618697,-0.001255742,0.032816716,0.012986315,-0.0022430478,0.011145998,0.013203705,0.022657583,0.009977959,-0.029956432,-0.014951027,-0.006765167,0.0063752695,-0.008393902,0.028672036,-0.021623181,0.027148986,-0.006116653,0.0079706265,-0.0035861556,0.0029689618,-0.00033138748,-0.0069160094,0.011602978,-0.031938408,-0.19010718,-0.020357963,-0.032763798,-0.021382598,-0.020173429,0.021584684,0.0015834359,0.0016991958,0.016665485,0.0049964557,-0.011903155,0.01637986,7.7104494e-05,0.033487514,-0.00026022657,-0.025586257,-0.014833268,0.012176407,0.019546092,0.0019491889,-0.024923036,-0.0067063235,0.012147735,-0.0017743051,0.0075207036,-