bf6f1ba8-7f36-4f65-9659-521bdceb83d9	[Context: lifestyle > weight_management | cdc]\nWhat to know\n\nTwo ways to find out if you're at a healthy weight: body mass index (BMI) and waist circumference.\nLosing weight doesn't have to mean losing a lot of weight.\nWillpower isn't the long-term answer for weight loss—read on to find out what is.\n\nWhat is a healthy weight?\n\nIs it the weight you think you should be? The same as you weighed 20 years ago? Ten pounds less than your sister-in-law? We can't provide an exact number for you. But we can give you some pointers on how to get to a healthy weight and stay there.\n\nTwo ways to get an idea if your weight is healthy or not: body mass index (BMI) and waist circumference.\n\nBMI\n\nBMI measures your height compared to your weight. For example, a person who is 5'7" and weighs 170 pounds has a BMI of 26.6, which is in the overweight range.\n\nBMI ranges\n\nUnderweight: less than 18.5Normal: 18.5–24.9Overweight: 25–29.9Obesity: 30 or greater\n\nBut experts note that BMI doesn't measure belly fat, and that's important. Too much belly fat can increase your risk for type 2 diabetes, heart disease, and stroke.\n\nWaist circumference\n\nWaist circumference (waist size) takes belly fat into account. It helps predict your risk of health problems from being overweight. Women whose waist measures more than 35 inches are at higher risk. So are men whose waist measures more than 40 inches. Losing weight can reduce belly fat and lower that risk!\n\nTo measure your waist correctly, stand and place a tape measure around your middle, just above your hip bones. Breathe in, and then measure your waist just after you breathe out.\n\nGetting started\n\nLosing weight doesn't have to mean losing a lot of weight. Taking off just 5% to 10% can improve your health and well-being. That's 10 to 20 pounds for a 200-pound person.\n\nIf you have diabetes, you may find your blood sugar levels are easier to manage after you lose weight. You may also need less diabetes medicine. Many people who lose weight notice that they have more energy and sleep better too.\n\nA healthy weight goal is one thing; dropping the pounds is quite another.	{"url": "https://www.cdc.gov/diabetes/living-with/healthy-weight.html", "title": "Weight Management", "topic": "weight_management", "source": "Centers for Disease Control and Prevention", "category": "lifestyle", "file_path": "lifestyle/weight_management/clean/cdc/v1.md", "chunk_index": 0, "total_chunks": 4, "source_org_code": "cdc", "parent_document_id": "c2873116-83b3-478d-b157-15cc26ee9160"}	[-0.008752469,-0.033019435,0.039580356,-0.030418597,0.015733501,0.010988107,0.020777864,0.0007112347,0.029927783,0.010219979,0.010222403,-0.019612579,-0.002895364,-0.005215629,0.114647925,0.031316146,0.021897435,-0.012885937,0.020381823,0.014361263,-0.0024459006,0.0130752865,0.020083116,-0.008727819,-0.008023073,-0.006729504,0.00065399904,0.004442137,0.02723317,0.035638638,0.024782656,0.0012319272,0.016176624,0.008668847,0.019462692,0.004928266,0.007087357,-0.0061734477,0.0033550141,-0.0047484133,-0.004160523,-0.0035856897,0.0044050226,-0.0012761655,0.017093796,-0.014570026,-0.013087906,0.0030802,0.015414663,0.014810269,-0.012322393,-0.0058296057,-0.015070669,-0.18538995,-0.025580917,-0.0050771385,-0.007795834,-0.021063013,-0.014164962,0.008602529,-0.024214063,0.02203434,0.00092228985,0.0008640646,0.009645493,-0.020718861,0.020802304,0.0024272152,-0.014150431,-0.031057056,0.031278286,0.0012513772,-0.008533386,-0.0035786284,-0.026516465,-0.02309635,-0.0132817505,0.019496271,0.008343642,-0.0065206513,-0.037389573,-0.01954435,-0.0010113574,-0.027452094,-0.019967066,0.0036577967,-0.0043035247,0.009427453,0.0061344826,-0.0018854189,0.0044480073,0.02544542,0.041016612,0.0140016675,0.028725382,-0.0076310914,-0.011394487,-0.018768907,0.004595813,-0.0015902678,-0.042592812,-0.026602767,0.017808,0.020419013,-0.007307558,0.023785142,-0.012424316,0.011984717,0.02123111,0.015546548,0.026447712,-0.012252