f022bea1-c2fc-4b72-a22e-09e9a48c8dad	[Context: lipid > total_cholesterol | medlineplus]\nHigh levels of triglycerides may increase the risk of heart disease, especially in women.\n\nThere is another type of cholesterol called VLDLÂ (very low-density lipoprotein). Some people also call VLDL a "bad" cholesterol because it contributes to the buildup of plaque in your arteries. But VLDL and LDL are different; VLDL mainly carries triglycerides, and LDL mainly carries cholesterol. VLDL isn't usually included in routine cholesterol tests because it's difficult to measure. Because VLDL contains a certain percentage of triglycerides, a lab can use your triglycerides level to estimate your VLDL level.\n\nWhy do I need a cholesterol test?\n\nYour provider may order a cholesterol test as part of a routine exam. When and how often you should get a cholesterol test depends on your age, risk factors, and family history. The general recommendations are:\n\nFor people who are age 19 or younger:\n\nThe first test should be between ages 9 to 11\nChildren should have the test again every 5 years\nSome children may have this test starting at age 2 if there is a family history of high cholesterol, heart attack, or stroke\n\nFor people who are ages 20 to 65:\n\nYounger adults should have the test every 5 years\nMen ages 45 to 65 and women ages 55 to 65 should have it every 1 to 2 years\n\nFor people older than 65:\n\nThey should be tested every year\n\nYou may also have a cholesterol test more often if you are at high risk of heart problems because of:\n\nA family history of heart disease\n\nHigh blood pressure\n\nType 2 diabetes\n\nSmoking\nExcess weight or obesity\n\nLack of physical activity\nA diet high in saturated fat\n\nWhat happens during a cholesterol test?\n\nA health care professional will take a blood sample from a vein in your arm, using a small needle. After the needle is inserted, a small amount of blood will be collected into a test tube or vial. You may feel a little sting when the needle goes in or out. This usually takes less than five minutes.\n\nYou may be able to use an at-home kitÂ to check your cholesterol levels. Your kit will include a device to prick your finger to collect a drop of blood for testing. Be sure to follow the kit instructions carefully.	{"url": "https://medlineplus.gov/lab-tests/cholesterol-levels/", "title": "Total Cholesterol", "topic": "total_cholesterol", "source": "MedlinePlus", "category": "lipid", "file_path": "lipid/total_cholesterol/clean/medlineplus/v1.md", "chunk_index": 1, "total_chunks": 8, "source_org_code": "medlineplus", "parent_document_id": "b114f73a-d3b4-406d-9334-8ebe004c3498"}	[-0.00637967,-0.0018841343,0.0021396158,-0.06104,0.010515917,-0.0141260335,0.01542718,0.036861166,0.0054946556,-0.0075787343,0.013994861,0.009724181,-0.005879982,-0.0041513466,0.108403966,0.02778605,0.016661055,-0.010969836,-0.007282224,-0.01719412,0.0073891655,0.01566333,0.00873481,-0.008745933,-0.010862594,-0.011491722,0.00809247,0.0007798442,0.040306482,0.021071939,0.0025424375,-0.03348906,0.013137411,0.015742213,0.010391755,0.015043151,0.005449664,-0.007112519,0.00086127844,0.005169002,0.00019305678,0.0016940407,-0.020718897,-0.0051715192,0.030109875,-0.022295345,-0.020995475,-0.0064944434,0.021876197,0.0030785478,0.0006707462,0.0089498935,-0.012049229,-0.1818172,0.015013385,0.0016537994,-0.0032574513,-0.027910773,0.006949589,-0.015714386,-0.031984713,0.0051861834,-0.011666538,-0.015611399,-0.013385905,-0.024531433,0.025267107,5.796918e-05,-0.027159335,-0.007905476,0.02291103,0.044422004,-0.00082202273,-0.016617866,-0.019139022,-0.025737854,-0.017975004,-0.0024176699,-0.0063985647,-0.0071951183,-0.019478157,-0.0031937186,0.007064319,-0.027600814,-0.004592291,0.0031857511,-0.0010414938,-0.008090922,-0.011037715,0.013576091,-0.0069566006,0.02200174,0.022110518,0.023714785,-0.008184738,0.02368529,0.021002477,-0.011485324,-0.0008905516,-0.0015124563,-0.00395641,-0.009645226,0.0072830166,-0.011793502,-0.0022718192,0.