e62a904f-41b7-41eb-ba36-9956a669161e	[Context: thyroid > t3 | medlineplus]\nBut if your TSH and free T4 levels are also normal, you probably don't have a thyroid condition.\nHigh T3 levels (free or total) may mean you have hyperthyroidism (too much thyroid hormone). High T3 levels with:\n\nLow TSH and high free T4 levels may mean that a problem with your thyroid gland is causing too much thyroid hormone.Normal or high TSH and high free T4 levels may mean that a pituitary gland condition is causing too much thyroid hormone.\nLow T3 levels (free or total) may mean hypothyroidism (too little thyroid hormone). Low or normal T3 levels with:\n\nHigh TSH levels and low free T4 levels may mean that a problem with your thyroid gland is causing too little thyroid hormone.Low TSH and low free T4 levels may mean that a pituitary gland condition is causing too little thyroid hormone.\n\nIf you have questions about your results, talk with your provider.\n\nLearn more about laboratory tests, reference ranges, and understanding results.\n\nReferences\n\nAmerican Thyroid Association [Internet]. Falls Church (VA): American Thyroid Association; c2023. Thyroid Function Tests; [cited 2023 Jun 14]; [about 9 screens]. Available from: https://www.thyroid.org/thyroid-function-tests\nARUP Consult [Internet]. Salt Lake City (UT): ARUP Laboratories; c2023. Initial Evaluation of Thyroid Function; [updated 2023 Feb; cited 2023 Jun 12]; [about 6 screens]. Available from: https://arupconsult.com/content/initial-evaluation-thyroid-function\nCleveland Clinic: Health Library: Diagnostics & Testing [Internet]. Cleveland (OH): Cleveland Clinic; c2023.T3 Triiodothyronine Test; [reviewed 2022 Feb 14; cited 2023 Jun 12]; [about 11 screens]. Available from: https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diagnostics/22425-triiodothyronine-t3\nMichigan Medicine: University of Michigan [Internet]. Ann Arbor (MI): Regents of the University of Michigan; c1995-2025. What is hyperthyroidism?; [cited 2025 Nov 19]; [about 4